KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp26
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ an exception thrown and caught in a constant expression: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
C++26 permits an exception object in a constant expression when it is
destroyed within the evaluation ([expr.const]/10.22).  CBMC's constant
evaluator does not evaluate throw and try: W<f()> fails to type-check
(CONVERSION ERROR), and `static_assert(f() == 4)' passes silently (an
assertion whose condition cannot be evaluated is not checked, with a
warning).  Checked with GCC 16.
